    {
      "id": "b-metric-decomposition-extremely-useful-in-intervi",
      "title": "B. Metric decomposition — extremely useful in interviews",
      "prose": [
        "Break top-line outcomes into the factors that create them. Use compatible denominators and time windows.",
        "Transaction volume",
        "Completed transactions = Active transactors × Attempts per transactor × Completion rate",
        "If completed volume falls: Are fewer people transacting, are they attempting fewer transactions, or are more attempts failing?",
        "Onboarding activation",
        "Activated users = Eligible starters × Onboarding completion rate × First-value rate",
        "Illustrative direct deposit funnel: Eligible account → Views switching instructions → Starts switch → Submits required details → First deposit received → Recurring deposit established.",
        "For primary banking, track recurring income, outgoing activity, and retention separately before combining them into an explicit primary-account definition."
      ],
      "table": {
        "columns": [
          "Stage-level rates must use compatible populations and time windows.",
          "Revenue"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Revenue = Active customers × Revenue per active customer",
            "Decompose further into usage frequency, monetized volume, and yield where appropriate."
          ],
          [
            "Funnel completion",
            "End-to-end conversion = Product of stage conversion rates"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "footnotes": [
        "A large decline in one stage can disproportionately affect the overall journey."
      ]
    },
